[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Identifying Assumptions and Biases in Critical Thinking"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In software engineering, data analysis, and system architecture, our decisions are only as good as the reasoning behind them. Identifying assumptions and biases is a core critical thinking competency that prevents catastrophic design flaws, security vulnerabilities, and logic errors. This tutorial explores how to programmatically and systematically evaluate your own mental models and technical requirements."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Defining Assumptions vs. Biases"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An assumption is an unexamined premise taken for granted as true without concrete proof. A bias is a systematic error in thinking that affects how we process information and make decisions. Recognizing the difference is the first step toward robust technical analysis."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Assumption: Assuming users will always have a stable high-speed internet connection."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bias (Confirmation Bias): Only reviewing error logs that confirm your hypothesis while ignoring logs that contradict it."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Systematic Identification Framework"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To systematically uncover hidden assumptions in system design or code architecture, apply the 'Inversion Technique'. Ask yourself: 'What must be true for this architecture to fail?'"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"def audit_system_assumptions(architecture_specs):
    hidden_assumptions = []
    for spec in architecture_specs:
        if 'always' in spec.lower() or 'never' in spec.lower():
            hidden_assumptions.append(spec)
    return hidden_assumptions"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Common Biases in Technical Decision Making"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Anchoring Bias: Relying too heavily on the first piece of information encountered (e.g., the first database technology you learned)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Availability Heuristic: Overestimating the likelihood of events based on their vividness or recent memory (e.g., designing exclusively for a recent security breach)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sunk Cost Fallacy: Continuing to maintain a legacy codebase simply because of the massive time already invested."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mitigating assumptions and biases requires continuous peer review, rigorous edge-case testing, and embracing a culture of psychological safety where questioning the status quo is encouraged. Always document your premises explicitly in your Technical Design Documents (TDD)."}]
